<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Every habit down one side, the last few days across — the dashboard's grid at
  widget size, read-only. Built from the same four colour roles
  `widget_habit.xml` and `widget_stats.xml` use, so a cell here and a cell in
  the app read as the same thing.

  FIXED views, hidden beyond need, exactly as `widget_stats.xml` does with its
  seven `stats_cell_*` cells. Not a `Bitmap`: one would have to be re-rendered
  on every resize, theme change and density change, and it carries a marshalled
  size limit — a grid of tinted `ImageView`s needs none of that. So the layout
  holds `Widgets.MAX_OVERVIEW_ROWS` rows of `Widgets.MAX_STRIP_DAYS` cells and
  `OverviewWidget.render` sets the surplus GONE, which is also what lets a
  resize reveal them again without re-inflating.

  MAX_OVERVIEW_ROWS is 7 and not 8 BECAUSE OF LINT. This is ~75 views (1 root +
  1 note + 1 header + 1 spacer + 7 day labels + 7 row containers + 7 names + 49
  cells + 1 footer); `TooManyViews` warns past 80, and an eighth row would be
  ~85. Adding a row means re-deriving `Widgets.overviewRows`'s thresholds, not
  raising a lint baseline.

  This widget follows the LAUNCHER's light/dark configuration, never the
  account's `theme` setting — `res/values-night/colors.xml` is the only
  `values-night` in the app. Same decision as the other two widgets, written
  down here rather than left as the default nobody chose.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/overview_root"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:padding="8dp"
    android:background="@drawable/widget_background">

    <!--
      The note line: the empty-account message, or "as of <date>" when the
      records have not been refreshed today — in which case today's column has
      no data and paints as UNKNOWN, which is indistinguishable from "answered
      nothing today". Visible for those two cases only. It has to be a REAL
      view and not a content description: the first version of the checkmark
      widget's own gone note was spoken and nowhere else, and the cell was then
      pixel-identical to a live one (docs/decisions/android.md).
    -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/overview_note"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:maxLines="1"
        android:ellipsize="end"
        android:textSize="10sp"
        android:textColor="@color/widget_text"
        android:paddingBottom="2dp"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <!--
      The day header. The first child is the name column's spacer — a bare
      TextView rather than a `Space`, which is not one of the view classes
      RemoteViews will inflate. The labels are narrow weekday initials written
